{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"1327 (THE FLIP) IS FULLY GREEN: LLM-Review=+1 [PASS], Verified=+1 (run 30862116263 SUCCESS), unresolved comments 0. It is NOT submitted — held until the ordering criterion's item (c) is observed. Its own review provenance is a SECOND pre-cutover Anthropic datapoint: artifact 76e8-4060-387f-4fe6, provider_provenance provider='anthropic' model='anthropic:claude-sonnet-4-6' tier='first_class', 0 blocking / 2 advisory / 0 dropped.\n\nRESPONDING TO BOTH ADVISORIES rather than banking the +1 and moving on. Both are correct.\n\nADVISORY 1 — scope-intent, severity minor, validity 0.71, at docker-compose.yml:212. Finding: REBAR_USAGE_LOG is a FOURTH env var, and this ticket's Scope bullet narrows the compose change to 'the three class-slot env vars … Region vars are already present and are not re-added'. So the var is neither a class slot nor a region var, and the Scope text does not cover it.\nACCEPTED AS A DOCUMENTATION DEFECT IN THIS TICKET, NOT AS SCOPE CREEP IN THE CHANGE. The var is required BY THIS TICKET'S OWN ACCEPTANCE CRITERION — the one demanding a REBAR_USAGE_LOG per-call tally showing every call on a bedrock: profile and ZERO direct-Anthropic calls. That criterion was unsatisfiable as written: usage recording is OPT-IN (usage_log.py's record() is a no-op with the var unset) and the var was set NOWHERE under infra/, so the production bot had never written a usage log. The Approach even anticipates the need ('Write the usage log to a durable path outside the job workspace') without ever saying to enable it. So the Scope bullet and the acceptance criteria contradicted each other, and the reviewer found the seam. REMEDY: I will amend the Scope bullet to name the usage-log enablement and why, rather than splitting a ticket to satisfy prose that is itself the error. Doing that as part of the single material edit before the pre-close review, so the attestation is not invalidated twice.\n\nADVISORY 2 — iac, severity minor, validity 0.89, at tests/unit/test_reviewbot_bedrock_cutover.py:88. Finding: the drift test compares the compose ids to the .tf's ModelId dimensions AS TEXT, which proves the alarm's CONFIG matches but not that the alarm EXISTS in CloudWatch; and it cites bug 1c39 — which I filed from this ticket — to note that terraform-drift never runs pre-submit on a Gerrit change, so a coupled alarm can land unapplied. Its checklist item: confirm the alarm is applied (state != INSUFFICIENT_DATA after an evaluation period) BEFORE the compose flip is submitted.\nALREADY DISCHARGED, and worth recording that the reviewer independently derived the exact check I had already run. Evidence, from the comment above: 1326 merged as 1f48330f2d; terraform plan from a clean worktree at that commit showed 'Plan: 1 to add, 0 to change, 0 to destroy'; applied exit 0; re-plan returns 'No changes'; and the alarm's own history records '16:33:22 StateUpdate Alarm updated from INSUFFICIENT_DATA to OK' — a transition that happened on real data, unforced, about a minute after creation. ARN arn:aws:cloudwatch:us-east-1:896586841071:alarm:rebar-bedrock-invoke-client-errors. So the alarm is applied and evaluating BEFORE 1327 is submitted, which is the ordering this advisory asks for.\nThe advisory is also a useful independent argument for why the text-level pin is still worth keeping: it guards the ids from DRIFTING APART, which is a different property from the alarm existing. Both checks are needed; neither substitutes for the other. I am NOT weakening the test in response.\n\nA SEPARATE OBSERVATION FROM THE PRODUCTION LOGS, recorded for follow-up and explicitly NOT this ticket's business: the review bot is logging, repeatedly, 'llm prompt caching requested for model=… but CANNOT engage: only 172 tokens sit ahead of the cache breakpoint, below this model's 1024-token minimum, while 15542 of the 15714 billed input tokens ride AFTER it unmarked — the provider declines a sub-minimum prefix silently (no error, cache_read=0 cache_write=0)'. That is a deliberate rebar diagnostic (src/rebar/llm/structured_run.py:490), not a silent failure, and it is firing on the code-review-coach pass, which is therefore paying full input price with zero cache reads on every review. It is a COST defect on the Anthropic path, unrelated to the provider seam, and I have not filed it because I have not yet confirmed whether it is already covered by the epic's now-closed caching bugs (f81d / e3cd). Flagging it so it is not lost."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785800800248171001,"uuid":"7c01a65c-8550-4a99-9f31-1e590069ae5b"}